{"proposal":{"arguments":[],"assumptions":[{"assumption_id":"node.481ce79be4fc0611266ee787.a0","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"Footnote 5's unqualified direction to confirm and demonstrate to the SFC has independent operative force.","status":"PROVISIONAL"},{"assumption_id":"node.481ce79be4fc0611266ee787.a1","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"This is a weaker rival because it must explain why paragraph 14 expressly conditions demonstration on a request.","status":"PROVISIONAL"},{"assumption_id":"node.481ce79be4fc0611266ee787.a2","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"The formalization selects activation of the minimum demonstration expectation, not its completion, due date or satisfactory performance.","status":"PROVISIONAL"}],"bundle_hash":"c7a0af73c7397b7ba7ba596ae90c5d7780a2e4c74c990995629ef97eef675307","controlled_language":"[TRUE_IS_SATISFIED] Within Product Provider scope, true means that the minimum demonstration expectation is activated without needing an SFC request; this proposed predicate is always true in scope and does not certify performance. Footnote 5 can be read as independently requiring confirmation and demonstration to the SFC of its stated minimum matters. Paragraph 14 retains the wider objects of soundness, ownership record keeping and integrity and its SFC satisfaction standard, but reconciling its request condition with this independent expectation remains the central uncertainty.","family_ids":["dependencies"],"parent_id":null,"revision_reason":"For a provider that has confirmed but has neither received a request nor demonstrated the minimum matters to the SFC, the preceding reading leaves demonstration untriggered; this reading treats minimum demonstration as independently expected. Absence of a stated deadline still prevents inferring that the provider is already overdue. The rival does not remove the separate request triggers in paragraphs 15–16.","source_packet_hash":"d74180e126dda06342060a17b37c3227fc695e9fc0f0d5ce68b187d7cb8bc612","source_